.TH BRK 2
.SH NAME
brk, sbrk, break \- change core allocation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B char *brk(char *addr);
.PP
.B char *sbrk(int incr);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Brk
sets the system's idea of the lowest location not used by the program
(called the break)
to
.I addr
(rounded up to the next page).
.B Note:
v7's page listed 64 bytes on the PDP11, 256 on the Interdata and 512 on the VAX.
.B The break is granted a page at a time here \- 1024 words, 6144 bytes \-
and both the kernel and b6sim round it up.
Locations not less than
.I addr
and below the stack pointer
are not in the address space and will thus
cause a memory violation if accessed.
.PP
In the alternate function
.I sbrk,
.I incr
more bytes are added to the
program's data space and a pointer to the
start of the new area is returned.
.PP
When a program begins execution via
.I exec
the break is set at the
highest location defined by the program
and data storage areas.
Ordinarily, therefore, only programs with growing
data areas need to use
.IR break .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
exec(2),
malloc(3),
end(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Zero is returned by
.I brk
if the break could be set;
\-1 if the program requests more
memory than the system limit.
.PP
.B Note:
.B sbrk fails with NULL, not (char *)\-1.
v7's value would have to be fabricated out of an integer, which is exactly what
the fat-pointer rule forbids \- see BESM-6 NOTES.  The break can never
legitimately be word 0, so NULL costs nothing.
.IR Malloc (3)
tests for NULL.
.PP
v7's remark about segmentation registers is PDP-11 hardware.  Here the kernel
refuses growth that would reach the stack at 070000.
.SH BUGS
.B Note:
v7's bug \- "setting the break in the range 0177701 to 0177777 on the PDP11 is
the same as setting it to zero" \- is a PDP-11 address wrap and does not arise.
The wrap that
.I does
arise here is described below.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS The break syscall takes a word address
A fat
.B char *
and a plain word address arrive at the gate as the same 15 bits, and
.B the byte offset is simply dropped.
So a mid-word pointer would floor the break to its word and hand back memory that
was never granted.  The library keeps the break as a real
.B char *
and converts the byte increment itself, calling the gate with a whole number of
words.
.SS Rounding is asymmetric on purpose
.I Sbrk
rounds
.I up
when growing and
.I toward zero
when shrinking.  The byte-to-word helper cannot do the second job: it is
(x + 5) / 6, and C truncates a negative quotient toward zero, so an exact multiple
of six would come up one word short.
.SS Growth past the address space wraps, quietly
A
.B char *
carries a 15-bit word address, so an increment past the end of the space does not
overflow \- it
.I wraps.
.I Sbrk
catches it by the only symptom it has:
.B growth that did not grow.
.SS `end' is declared as an array
.B "extern char end[];"
so that its decay produces a genuine fat pointer at byte offset 0, rather than a
cast.  See
.IR end (3).
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_break
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
